1 FORTRAN

   Compiles VAX FORTRAN source files into VAX MACRO-32 source files
   (.MAR).  MACRO assembles each into an object module, and LINK links
   the objects into an image RUN runs.

   Format

     FORTRAN filespec[{,|+}...]

2 Parameter

 filespec[{,|+}...]

   The source file, or several separated by commas, each compiled to its
   own MACRO file.  Files joined by + are one compilation, to one MACRO
   file named for the first, and a file followed by /LIBRARY is a text
   library (.TLB by default) for that compilation's INCLUDE statements.
   Without a file type a source is .FOR.  Files are found by MACRO's
   rules (see MACRO Parameter); quote a host path to keep its case.

2 Description

   A program compiles, links, and runs in four commands:

     $ FORTRAN SQUARES         ! SQUARES.FOR -> SQUARES.MAR
     $ MACRO SQUARES           ! SQUARES.MAR -> SQUARES.OBJ
     $ LINK SQUARES            ! SQUARES.OBJ -> SQUARES.EXE
     $ RUN SQUARES

   (FORTRAN/OBJECT runs MACRO too.)  The MACRO file is meant to be read:
   it holds the FORTRAN source as comments, each line with its number,
   so the code and the FORTRAN it came from are side by side.  A comment
   names each INCLUDE file, whose lines appear only with INCLUDE's
   /LIST, with their include depth before their numbers.

   The compiler reports each problem it finds with the line it is on:

     %FORT-E-NOTIMPL, the DICTIONARY statement is not implemented yet
       at line 7 of DUA1:[WORK]PROG.FOR;1
             DICTIONARY 'PARTS'
       ------^

   After an error (E) or a fatal error (F) no MACRO file is written, so
   a failed compilation never replaces a good one, and the command ends
   with DCL-E-FORERRORS.  After warnings (W) the file is written and
   $STATUS is a warning.  With several sources, each failure is shown as
   it happens and the others are still compiled.  The compiler checks
   the program units of one file against each other: a function's type
   where it is used, and the number of arguments of each call.

   From the host shell, "govax fortran filespec..." runs this command,
   with --macro file or --no-macro, --extend-source, --d-lines,
   --continuations n, --no-i4, --g-floating, --align value, --no-align,
   --optimize, --no-optimize, --list, --list-file file, --show keywords,
   --cross-reference, --check keywords, --warnings keywords, --standard
   keywords, --debug keywords, --object, --object-file file, --no-f77,
   and --recursive.

2 Language

   The compiler takes VAX FORTRAN V5.0, FORTRAN-77 with DEC's
   extensions: main programs, subroutines, functions of every type,
   ENTRY, and BLOCK DATA; declarations of every type, IMPLICIT,
   PARAMETER, arrays, COMMON, EQUIVALENCE, DATA, SAVE, EXTERNAL, and
   INTRINSIC; DEC's STRUCTURE, RECORD, UNION, and MAP, octal,
   hexadecimal, Hollerith, and RADIX-50 constants, VOLATILE, AUTOMATIC,
   and STATIC, and the CDEC$ directives (IDENT, PSECT, TITLE, SUBTITLE,
   OPTIONS, and END OPTIONS); assignment and expressions in every type
   and length (BYTE, LOGICAL, INTEGER, REAL in F, D or G, and H, COMPLEX,
   and CHARACTER, with substrings and concatenation), and every
   intrinsic function, in line or by MTHRTL; every IF, DO, and GO TO,
   CALL, RETURN, STOP, and PAUSE; READ, WRITE, and the other I/O
   statements, and OPEN and the other file statements, on sequential,
   relative, and indexed files, the keyed READ with every key specifier
   among them.  DICTIONARY draws %FORT-E-NOTIMPL.

   From a later DEC FORTRAN, a FUNCTION or SUBROUTINE may be RECURSIVE
   (RECURSIVE INTEGER FUNCTION FACT(N) RESULT(F)), as may every one in a
   file under /RECURSIVE or after OPTIONS /RECURSIVE.  Such a routine may
   call itself, and each activation has its own variables, but for those
   SAVE'd, given initial values, in COMMON, or STATIC, and its own
   temporaries, so an AST or a handler may also enter it while it runs.
   In the routine, its name with an argument list is a call.  Without
   RESULT the name alone is the function's value; RESULT(name) names
   another variable for it instead.  AUTOMATIC alone doesn't make a
   routine safe to enter twice: its temporaries stay static.

   A program can use VMS directly: a system service or RTL routine is a
   routine like any other, called as a function for its status, with
   arguments left out (STATUS = SYS$GETJPIW(,,,LIST,IOSB,,)), %VAL,
   %REF, %DESCR, and %LOC.  A FORTRAN function can be a condition
   handler (LIB$ESTABLISH), and a subroutine an AST routine or exit
   handler.  The system subroutines are there: DATE, TIME, IDATE, EXIT,
   SECNDS, RAN, MVBITS, and ERRSNS.

2 Programs

   A program may be several files, each compiled and assembled on its
   own, then linked together.  A common block of one name is the same
   storage in each unit that declares it, as long as the longest
   declaration.  A BLOCK DATA may give it initial values from another
   file; name the BLOCK DATA in an EXTERNAL statement, so that LINK
   reports it if it's missing and finds it in a library:

     $ FORTRAN STORE,SHOW,START
     $ MACRO STORE,SHOW,START
     $ LINK STORE,SHOW,START

   INCLUDE 'file' reads a file, by default of type .FOR, found relative
   to the file that includes it: on the host, in its directory; on a
   volume, on its device and in its directory.  INCLUDE 'file/LIST' puts
   the file's lines in the MACRO file; /NOLIST is the default.

   INCLUDE '(module)' reads a text library's module (see LIBRARY), from
   the first of these that has it: the libraries given with the source
   (FORTRAN PROG+MYLIB/LIBRARY), the library the logical name
   FORT$LIBRARY names, SYS$LIBRARY:FORSYSDEF.TLB, and last the definition
   modules built into govax, such as $SSDEF, $IODEF, $JPIDEF, $FORDEF,
   $CHFDEF, and $SYSSRVNAM, whose INTEGER*4 and PARAMETER statements give
   VMS's names their values.  INCLUDE 'lib(module)' reads only that
   library.

2 FTNRTL

   Compiled programs call FTNRTL, govax's FORTRAN run-time library, a
   shareable image written in MACRO-32 and built into govax.  LINK finds
   its routines (named FTN$...) without an options file, and RUN finds
   the image.  Both look for a file of one's own first: a logical name
   FTNRTL, SYS$SHARE:FTNRTL.EXE, or FTNRTL.EXE in the host library
   directory.  The system volume has its sources, and
   SYS$SOURCES:BUILDFTN.COM, which rebuilds it.

   FTNRTL has FTN$VERSION, FTN$STOP, the I/O statements' calls
   (FTN$READ_SF, FTN$WRITE_SF, and the other begin calls, FTN$IO_ITEM,
   FTN$IO_END), ISHFTC (FTN$IISHFTC, FTN$JISHFTC), and a routine for each
   intrinsic function MTHRTL lacks, for a program that passes the
   function's name as an argument (FTN$LEN, FTN$INDEX, ...,
   FTN$NWORKERS), which MACRO programs can call too.

   List-directed output goes to SYS$OUTPUT in records of 80 bytes, each
   starting with a blank (its carriage control), each value in its
   type's format: LOGICAL as T or F; an integer in 5 columns for BYTE
   and LOGICAL*1, 7 for INTEGER*2, and 12 for INTEGER*4; REAL with 7
   significant digits, REAL*8 with 16 (15 with /G_FLOATING), REAL*16
   with 33; COMPLEX as its two parts in parentheses, as
   (1.000000,-2.500000); and CHARACTER as its text.

2 Qualifiers
/[NO]ALIGN[=value]

   How structures and common blocks are laid out.  /NOALIGN, the
   default, packs them, as VAX FORTRAN V5.0 does.  /ALIGN puts each
   field and common name on its natural boundary.  A value is ALL, NONE,
   NATURAL, PACKED, or class=rule, and several go in parentheses: the
   classes are RECORDS (or STRUCTURES) and COMMONS, and the rules
   PACKED, NATURAL, and for COMMONS STANDARD, at most a longword.  /ALIGN
   alone is ALL.  CDEC$ OPTIONS groups in the source override it.

/[NO]CHECK[=(keyword[,...])]

   What the compiled code looks out for: ALL, NONE, [NO]BOUNDS
   (subscripts and substrings: the subscript range trap; an adjustable
   array's bounds: FOR$_ADJARRDIM), [NO]OVERFLOW (integer overflow
   traps), and [NO]UNDERFLOW (floating underflow faults).  /CHECK alone
   is ALL.  The default is /NOCHECK.  An OPTIONS statement before a unit
   may change it for that unit.

/CONTINUATIONS=n

   The most continuation lines one statement may have, from 0 to 99.
   The default is 19.

/[NO]CROSS_REFERENCE

   A listing follows each unit's map with where its names and labels
   are mentioned: a line number each, with D if the line defines the
   name and A if it assigns it; 1:3 is line 3 of a file included at
   depth 1.  It is there after errors too.  Without /LIST it does
   nothing but say so.  The default is /NOCROSS_REFERENCE.

/[NO]D_LINES

   Compiles the debugging lines (those with a D in column 1) as
   statements.  The default is /NOD_LINES: they are comments.

/[NO]DEBUG[=(keyword[,...])]

   What MACRO is to write for the debugger: ALL, NONE, [NO]SYMBOLS
   (debugger records), and [NO]TRACEBACK (a traceback's names).  /DEBUG
   alone is ALL, so a plain MACRO then writes debugger records.
   /NODEBUG, the default, is traceback alone.  It doesn't turn the
   optimizer off: give /NOOPTIMIZE as well to debug.

/[NO]EXTEND_SOURCE

   Lets the statement field of a fixed-format line run to column 132
   instead of column 72.  The default is /NOEXTEND_SOURCE.  An OPTIONS
   statement overrides this for the unit after it.

/[NO]F77

   /NOF77 is FORTRAN-66's reading of what FORTRAN-77 changed: a DO runs
   at least once, OPEN's defaults are STATUS='NEW' and BLANK='ZERO', and
   EXTERNAL means a library function of that name unless written *name.
   The default is /F77.  An OPTIONS statement before a unit may change it
   for that unit.

/[NO]G_FLOATING

   REAL*8 (DOUBLE PRECISION) values are G_floating: 53 bits of
   precision, about 15 decimal digits, and a range of about 0.56E-308 to
   0.9E308.  With /NOG_FLOATING, the default, they are D_floating: 56
   bits, about 16 digits, and REAL's range, about 0.29E-38 to 1.7E38.  A
   unit's OPTIONS statement overrides this for that unit; the units of a
   program must agree, since a value passed from one format to the other
   is misread.

/HOST

   After a source, makes it a host path, whatever it looks like.

/[NO]I4

   INTEGER and LOGICAL with no length, and implied integers, are 4 bytes
   (the default).  With /NOI4 they are 2, and an integer constant from
   -32768 to 32767 is an INTEGER*2.  A unit's OPTIONS statement
   overrides this for that unit.

/LIBRARY

   After a source joined to others by +, makes it a text library for
   that compilation's INCLUDE '(module)' statements.

/[NO]LIST[=filespec]

   Writes a listing: the source's lines with their numbers, each message
   under its line, and each unit's storage map.  By default it is the
   source's name with the type LIS, beside the source, and it is written
   after errors too.  The default is /NOLIST.  CDEC$ TITLE and SUBTITLE
   set the heading of the page after the one they are on; with no
   TITLE, a page's title is its unit's name.

/[NO]MACRO[=filespec]

   Writes the MACRO file (the default).  Without a file name, it has the
   source's name and location, with the type MAR in the same case as
   the source's type: squares.for gives squares.mar.  A name is placed
   as MACRO's /OBJECT places its object, and a VMS name without a type
   gets MAR.  On a volume, the file is the next version of its name.  A
   host source whose type is already MAR is refused unless /MACRO names
   another file, since its MACRO file would replace it.  /NOMACRO
   compiles the source and reports any errors, but writes no MACRO file.

/[NO]OBJECT[=filespec]

   /OBJECT runs MACRO on the MACRO file too, for an object file, by
   default the source's name with type OBJ.  The MACRO file is kept, so
   /OBJECT can't go with /NOMACRO.  The default is /NOOBJECT.

/[NO]OPTIMIZE

   Runs the optimizer's passes, for code that does what the program
   says in fewer instructions.  The passes run are named in the MACRO
   file's header.  The default is /OPTIMIZE.

/[NO]RECURSIVE

   Every FUNCTION and SUBROUTINE may be active more than once at a time,
   as if each said RECURSIVE: its local variables are in its stack frame
   unless SAVE'd, given initial values, in COMMON, or STATIC.  The
   default is /NORECURSIVE.  An OPTIONS statement before a unit may
   change it for that unit.

/[NO]SHOW[=(keyword[,...])]

   What a listing holds: ALL, NONE, [NO]INCLUDE (every INCLUDE file's
   lines, whatever INCLUDE's own /LIST says), and [NO]MAP.  /SHOW alone
   is ALL and /NOSHOW NONE; with no /SHOW, a listing has the maps, and
   only the INCLUDE files that say /LIST.

/[NO]STANDARD[=(keyword[,...])]

   Flags what FORTRAN-77 doesn't have, each an informational message, so
   the program still compiles: SYNTAX (statements, constants, operators,
   specifiers, edit descriptors, names, intrinsic functions),
   SOURCE_FORM (tabs, small letters, ! comments, debugging lines, past
   column 72, over 19 continuation lines), ALL, NONE, and NOSYNTAX and
   NOSOURCE_FORM.  /STANDARD alone is ALL.  The default is /NOSTANDARD.

/[NO]WARNINGS[=(keyword[,...])]

   What the compiler says, beyond errors: ALL, NONE, [NO]GENERAL (its
   warnings and informational messages; on by default),
   [NO]DECLARATIONS (each name with no type of its own), [NO]UNUSED
   (names only declared, and labels nothing refers to), and
   [NO]ALIGNMENT (fields and common names off their natural
   boundaries).  /WARNINGS alone is ALL and /NOWARNINGS is NONE.  What
   isn't shown doesn't count: a compilation with only those ends with a
   good status.

2 Examples

   1.$ FORTRAN SQUARES

     Compiles SQUARES.FOR into SQUARES.MAR.

   2.$ FORTRAN DUA1:[SRC]PROG /MACRO=DUA1:[OBJ]

     Writes PROG.MAR in another directory.

   3.$ FORTRAN A,B,C

     Compiles three files, each to its own MACRO file.

   4.$ FORTRAN PROG+DEFS/LIBRARY

     Compiles PROG.FOR, its INCLUDE '(module)' statements reading
     DEFS.TLB first.

   5.$ FORTRAN PROG /ALIGN=(RECORDS=NATURAL,COMMONS=STANDARD)

     Lays out structures naturally, and common blocks at most on
     longwords.

   6.$ FORTRAN PROG /LIST /CROSS_REFERENCE /SHOW=INCLUDE

     Writes PROG.LIS with a cross reference and every INCLUDE file's
     lines.

   7.$ FORTRAN PROG /CHECK=BOUNDS /WARNINGS=(DECLARATIONS,UNUSED)

     Checks subscripts as the program runs, and reports names without
     types and names never used.

   8.$ FORTRAN/DEBUG/NOOPTIMIZE/OBJECT PROG
     $ LINK/DEBUG PROG
     $ RUN PROG

     Builds PROG for the debugger, and runs it under the debugger.

   9.% govax --mount-write DUA1=work.dsk fortran DUA1:[000000]PROG.FOR

     Compiles a volume's file from the host shell.
